## 2. Dependencies — see `release/SBOM.json`

The candidate artifact uses only the dependencies listed in
`release/SBOM.json` (103 components: 33 Python, 70 Node), with
name / version / licence / source for each. Licence families present:

- MIT, MIT-0, ISC, PSF-2.0 (permissive)
- Apache-2.0, BSD-2-Clause, BSD-3-Clause (permissive, attribution)

No copyleft (GPL / LGPL / AGPL) dependency is present in the SBOM.
If a future dependency introduces a copyleft licence, it must be
reviewed before it is added to any lock file — this NOTICE and the
SBOM must be updated in the same change.

Runtime note: the Python core is stdlib + SQLite only and has zero
runtime Python dependencies. The MCP server uses the official MCP
Python SDK (`mcp` 2.3.0, MIT) plus its transitive dependencies.
The local workbench HTTP API is stdlib `http.server` only — no
FastAPI / Uvicorn at runtime (Uvicorn appears in the SBOM only as a
transitive dependency of the MCP SDK). The UI is built with Vite /
TypeScript / Playwright (dev / build / test only).

System tools: CPython 3.12.3 (PSF-2.0), bundled SQLite 3.45.1
(public domain), Node.js 24.22.1 (MIT, build/test only).
Graphviz is **not** used anywhere in this repository and is not
part of the candidate artifact.

## 3. What is NOT bundled

- No third-party full text is packaged: `docs/comparison.md` and
  `docs/COMPETITORS.md` quote only short competitor taglines and
  link to the original projects; those projects keep their own
  licences and the quotations do not relicense them.
- The kit's distribution note names Relay (BSL) only as a
  comparison point: BSL is **not** an OSI open-source licence and
  no Relay code is included. Turnkeep's own code is MIT; nothing
  in this repository should be described as "open source" on the
  strength of merely being source-visible. Until the repository is
  actually published under the MIT licence in `LICENSE`, the
  accurate description is "MIT-licensed source, release candidate
  for local use" — see `SECURITY.md` and `docs/KNOWN_LIMITATIONS.md`.
- No third-party demo assets, fonts, images or sample data are
  bundled. UI `dist/` output is built from the project's own
  TypeScript sources; the only image/svg files in the tree live
  inside `ui/node_modules/` (Playwright's own assets) and are
  never shipped (gitignored, excluded from the release package).

## 4. Known lock-file gaps (recorded, not hidden)

- `requirements/ui.lock` is empty/stale (it still says "T029 pins
  later") while `ui/package-lock.json` actually pins the Node
  dependencies. The SBOM's Node inventory comes from
  `ui/package-lock.json`. `ui.lock` must be regenerated or removed
  by its owner; T045 does not edit files outside its owned paths.
- `pyproject.toml` `[project.optional-dependencies]` still lists
  `mcp = []` and `ui = []`, while `requirements/mcp.lock` pins the
  real MCP dependencies. Same owner follow-up.
- Python transitive packages installed in `.venv` but absent from
  every `requirements/*.lock` are listed in the SBOM with source
  "transitive, not pinned in a lock" — real versions from installed
  package metadata, not estimates.
